---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'The pulsar command: themes, health checks, a diagnosis report, agent setup, updates and rollback. Reads work unprivileged; only the commands that change the system ask for root.';

// The CLI's own usage text, verbatim from cli/pulsar. A transcript, not a
// retyping: if the help changes, this is the place that has to change with it.
const USAGE = [
  "pulsar -- manage a Pulsar system",
  "",
  "  pulsar manifest          what is in this image, and what it is running on",
  "  pulsar doctor            health snapshot (exit 1 if a check fails);",
  "                           the default when no command is given",
  "  pulsar status            deployments: booted, staged, rollback, pins",
  "  pulsar changelog         packages that moved in the latest published build",
  "  pulsar sbom              this system's packages as SPDX 2.3",
  "  pulsar attest            print (and run) the provenance check for this image",
  "",
  "  pulsar update            fetch and stage an update        (root)",
  "                           through bootc, or through rpm-ostree if you have",
  "                           layered packages, which bootc would drop",
  "  pulsar update --check    is a newer image published? no root needed.",
  "                           exit 0 up to date or already staged, 10 available,",
  "                           1 could not tell. add --notify for a desktop",
  "                           notification, once per published build",
  "  pulsar flatpak-gl        do running Flatpak apps have the NVIDIA driver?",
  "                           an app started before its GL extension arrived",
  "                           renders games on the iGPU. --notify says so once",
  "  pulsar agents-md         print the guide a coding agent should read first",
  "  pulsar rollback          boot the previous deployment     (root)",
  "  pulsar pin | unpin       protect the booted deployment    (root)",
  "",
  "  pulsar report            diagnosis bundle to paste: doctor, status,",
  "                           manifest, failed units, warning+ journal lines,",
  "                           graphics, flatpak. JSON; --text for people.",
  "                           redacted; see: pulsar report --help",
  "",
  "  pulsar theme <command>   re-colour the whole desktop in one step:",
  "                           list | set <theme> | revert | ...  see: pulsar theme",
  "",
  "  pulsar setup <recipe>    apps | devbox | gamemode | quadlet | gamescale",
  "  pulsar setup agent <name>",
  "                           a coding agent CLI in a toolbox, run from the",
  "                           host: claude | codex | gemini | opencode | aider.",
  "                           none by default; --list, --remove <name>",
  "",
  "Options",
  "  --logo, --no-logo        force or suppress the mark in `manifest`",
  "  --json                   machine-readable output (doctor, status, manifest,",
  "                           changelog; report is JSON already); everything",
  "                           else ignores it.",
  "                           `manifest --json` adds the host facts under a",
  "                           \"host\" key, so `jq 'del(.host)'` is the baked",
  "                           manifest and two machines on the same build still",
  "                           compare equal",
  "  --version, --help",
];

const usage = USAGE.join('\n');

const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'The pulsar command',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': 'https://pulsar.arclight.digital/#pulsar' },
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Arclight Digital LLC', url: 'https://arclight.build' },
  },
];
const DEVBOX = 'pulsar setup devbox    # distrobox assemble from /usr/share/pulsar/distrobox.ini';
---

<Base title="The pulsar command — Pulsar’s CLI, explained" description={DESCRIPTION} path="/cli" jsonLd={JSONLD}>
  <main id="main" class="page">
    <arc-container size="lg">
      <arc-page-header border>
        <h1 slot="heading">The pulsar command</h1>
        <p slot="description">One command for everything you do with an immutable system: make it look like yours, check it’s healthy, see what changed, hand it to an agent, and undo it if you need to.</p>
      </arc-page-header>

      <section class="chapter">
        <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark usage" filename="pulsar --help" code={usage}></arc-code-block>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="theme">
        <h2>theme: the whole desktop, one step</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <p>
            <code>pulsar theme set nord</code> re-colours the accent, the apps, the terminal, the
            text editor, btop, the top bar, quick settings and the wallpaper together, as one change.{' '}
            <code>pulsar theme list</code> shows what ships; any base16 scheme imports beside them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>pulsar theme revert</code> puts stock GNOME back exactly. Apps that were already
            open are offered a restart, and each is asked to quit the way Ctrl+Q asks, so unsaved
            work keeps its dialog. Nothing is ever killed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="agents">
        <h2>For coding agents</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <p>
            <code>pulsar agents-md</code> prints the briefing the image ships for coding agents: how
            this system differs from a mutable one, and what not to touch.{' '}
            <code>pulsar setup agent claude</code> (or <code>codex</code>, <code>gemini</code>,{' '}
            <code>opencode</code>, <code>aider</code>) installs that agent into its own toolbox and
            runs it from any terminal. None ships by default, and one you installed yourself is left
            alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>pulsar report</code> is the one thing to paste when something is broken: health
            checks, failed services, recent warnings, the GPU and Flatpak state, as JSON or{' '}
            <code>--text</code>, with hostname, username and addresses redacted. What an agent can
            and cannot break is written down in{' '}
            <a href="https://github.com/arclight-digital/pulsar/blob/main/docs/AGENTS-SAFETY.md">AGENTS-SAFETY.md</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="doctor">
        <h2>Why doctor exists</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <p>
            <code>systemctl is-active scx.service</code> reported active for minutes at a stretch
            while no scheduler was attached and the machine ran stock EEVDF — unit state is not
            system state. So <code>doctor</code> reads <code>/sys/kernel/sched_ext/state</code> and
            the other places where the truth actually lives, and <code>--json</code> makes it
            scriptable. Only a <em>fail</em> sets a nonzero exit; a scheduler that did not attach
            merely warns, because the machine is entirely usable without one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Reads go through <code>rpm-ostree</code>, which works unprivileged; only the commands
            that change the system ask for root. <code>bootc status</code> needs root even to read,
            and a health check you need sudo for is one you will not run.{' '}
            <code>pulsar sbom</code> is generated, never baked — a file inside the image cannot
            describe the image containing it, so it reads the live rpm database, through the same
            script the nightly uses.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="update">
        <h2>update, not upgrade</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <p>
            Use <code>pulsar update</code> rather than <code>bootc upgrade</code> directly if you
            have layered anything with <code>rpm-ostree install</code>. bootc's model is the
            container image alone — it does not know about your layer and will not carry it forward.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>pulsar update</code> checks the booted deployment first and hands off to{' '}
            <code>rpm-ostree upgrade</code> when there is something to preserve;{' '}
            <code>pulsar doctor</code> tells you which side of that line you are on.{' '}
            <code>pulsar update --check</code> answers "is there a newer image" without root, and{' '}
            <code>--notify</code> turns that into a desktop notification, once per published build.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="setup">
        <h2>For development</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <p>
              <code>bpftrace</code>, <code>bcc-tools</code>, <code>sysstat</code> and{' '}
              <code>perf</code> are on the host because a container cannot attach probes to the
              host kernel. <code>mise</code> and <code>direnv</code> handle toolchains, so compilers
              and SDKs are pinned per project instead of layered into the image. A default dev box
              is one command away:
            </p>
            <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="devbox" code={DEVBOX}></arc-code-block>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>
              <code>podman-auto-update.timer</code> is enabled for user sessions, and a commented
              quadlet template lives at <code>/usr/share/pulsar/templates/example.container</code>{' '}
              — containers as systemd units, rootless, in a file you can version
              (<code>pulsar setup quadlet</code>).
            </p>
            <p>
              <code>pulsar setup gamescale</code> runs the installer copy staged in the image for
              Flatpak launchers — <a href="/gamescale#flatpak">why that step exists</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </arc-container>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .usage {
    display: block;
    max-width: 62rem;
  }
  .two {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    max-width: 72rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .two arc-code-block {
    display: block;
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0 var(--space-md);
  }
  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .two {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-md);
    }
  }
</style>
